INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Wirecard KPMG announcement and European Parliament/SAFE policy briefing", "source_url": "SRC-001; SRC-002", "text": "SRC-001 (Wirecard announcement, 28 April 2020): Wirecard said KPMG's special audit found no incriminating evidence for public allegations of balance-sheet forgery and no significant findings requiring adjustment of 2016-2018 annual accounts. It said KPMG could not obtain all data needed to prove revenues because data were controlled by third parties; Wirecard reported documentation and organizational weaknesses, and delayed annual accounts/auditor completion. SRC-002 (European Parliament ECON-commissioned SAFE policy briefing): authors assess that all five lines of defense failed in one way or another: Wirecard internal controls, supervisory board, external audit, financial-reporting/audit oversight bodies, and BaFin. The briefing says warning signals existed years earlier; FT reports and whistleblower information began in 2015; Zatarra report in 2016 alleged money laundering and fraud; BaFin requested FREP investigation in January 2019, restricted net short positions and filed complaints against short sellers and a journalist; KPMG was hired October 2019 and its delayed April 2020 report could not verify €1 billion in foreign bank accounts; BaFin filed a criminal complaint in June 2020 and Wirecard filed bankruptcy days later acknowledging €1.9 billion in missing financial assets. The paper labels its analysis as the authors' view and warns about hindsight bias. It recommends stronger audit accountability, internal controls, and BaFin powers/accountability. No benchmark source returned evidence about Jan Marsalek's disappearance, route, whereabouts, or alleged intelligence links; those points are omitted."}
